WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.800
An L two norm measures a function's size by integrating its squared magnitude and taking a square root

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.644
It treats the entire function as one vector

00:00:09.644 --> 00:00:15.333
For the function x on the unit interval, the norm is one over square root three.

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:23.783
A function equal to one on an interval of width one quarter and zero elsewhere has squared integral one quarter

00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:26.643
Its L two norm is therefore one half

00:00:26.643 --> 00:00:30.933
Both height and the measure of the supporting region determine the norm.

00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:35.290
Shrinking support alone need not shrink the norm

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:42.549
Set the height to square root n on the open interval zero to one over n, and zero elsewhere

00:00:42.549 --> 00:00:47.133
Squared area remains one, yet every fixed input eventually has value zero.

00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:53.622
Functions that differ only on a measure-zero set have zero L two distance

00:00:53.622 --> 00:00:57.215
The space therefore identifies them as the same vector

00:00:57.215 --> 00:01:04.400
A spike at just one point changes a drawn value but not the corresponding equivalence class or norm.
